# Console keymap -> xkb layout, and whether that layout can type '/' without
# SHIFT.
#
# THREE different vocabularies name the same keyboard and they do not agree:
# the console keymap (loadkeys, /etc/vconsole.conf), the xkb layout Wayland
# wants, and the locale. The installer asks once, in console-keymap terms,
# because that is what is available before there is a graphical session. This
# file is how that one answer reaches everything else.
#
# ONE table, read by bin/provision-arch.sh and by hypr/common/layout.lua. The
# mapping used to live only in the provisioner, so the compositor had no way to
# know the layout and every keybind was chosen as if the keyboard were American.
#
# Columns: <console keymap>  <xkb layout>  <slash-unshifted: yes|no>
#
# slash-unshifted is not decoration. On a US keyboard '/' is its own key; on
# the Latin American, Spanish, German and French layouts it is SHIFT+7 or
# similar, which means a keybind on SLASH cannot be pressed at all there -- the
# SHIFT the layout requires is a modifier the bind does not match. That is why
# the help keybind is chosen from this column rather than hardcoded.
#
# Only entries whose console and xkb names DIFFER, or whose slash answer is
# not the default, need a row. Anything absent maps to itself with
# slash-unshifted=no, which is the safe default: it picks a function key for
# help rather than one the user cannot press.

# console      xkb        slash-unshifted
us             us         yes
us-acentos     us         yes
gb             gb         yes
uk             gb         yes
dvorak         us         no
colemak        us         no

la-latin1      latam      no
latam          latam      no
es             es         no
br-abnt2       br         no
br-abnt        br         no
pt-latin1      pt         no
de-latin1      de         no
de             de         no
fr-latin9      fr         no
fr             fr         no
it             it         no
dk-latin1      dk         no
no-latin1      no         no
se-lat6        se         no
fi             fi         no
pl             pl         no
ru             ru         no
